---
description: Technical writing and documentation standards for our self-managed Markdown docs
version: 1.7.1
globs: [
  "docs/**",
  "apps/**/docs/**",
  "packages/**/docs/**",
  "**/*.md"
]
alwaysApply: false
---

Docs are plain Markdown, built into a static site by `scripts/build_docs.py` and
deployed to Cloudflare. Write portable Markdown only — no docs-platform-specific
components (no Mintlify/MDX components, JSX, or custom tags).

- Use clear, direct language; write in second person ("you"), active voice, and present tense.
- Lead with the most important information (inverted pyramid); break complex procedures into numbered steps with prerequisites and expected outcomes.
- Maintain consistent terminology; define jargon on first use and use parallel structure in lists and headings.
- Begin every page with YAML frontmatter containing `title` and `description` (both required — CI rejects pages missing either).
- Start body headings at `##` (the `title` frontmatter renders as the page `<h1>`); keep headings keyword-rich for navigation.
- Register every page in `docs/nav.json` so it appears in the sidebar; a nav entry without a matching file fails CI.
- Link between pages with relative `.md` links (e.g. `[usage](usage.md)`); the build rewrites them to `.html` and the link checker fails on missing targets or anchors.
- Use fenced code blocks with a language hint; provide complete, runnable examples with realistic data, proper error handling, and expected output. Never include real secrets.
- Store images under `docs/assets/` and reference them relatively; every image needs descriptive alt text (enforced by the checker). Use specific link text, never "click here".
- Use standard Markdown tables for structured comparisons and blockquotes for callouts/notes (they render as styled callouts in the theme).
- Validate and preview locally before pushing: `python3 scripts/check_docs_links.py` then `python3 scripts/build_docs.py`. See `docs/style-guide.md` for the full conventions.
